\section*{Question 5}

The derivative of a function \(f\) is given by \(f^{\prime}(x)=(x-3) e^{x}\) for \(x>0\), and \(f(1)=7\).
\begin{itemize}
\item[(a)] The function \(f\) has a critical point at \(x=3\). At this point, does \(f\) have a relative minimum, a relative maximum, or neither? Justify your answer.
\item[(b)] On what intervals, if any, is the graph of \(f\) both decreasing and concave up? Explain your reasoning.
\item[(c)] Find the value of \(f(3)\).
\item[(a)] \(f^{\prime}(x)<0\) for \(0<x<3\) and \(f^{\prime}(x)>0\) for \(x>3\)
Therefore, \(f\) has a relative minimum at \(x=3\).
\end{itemize}

2 : \(\left\{\begin{array}{l}\text { 1: minimum at } x=3 \\ \text { 1: justification }\end{array}\right.\)
\(3:\left\{\begin{array}{l}2: f^{\prime \prime}(x) \\ 1: \text { answer with reason }\end{array}\right.\)
\begin{itemize}
\item[(b)] \(f^{\prime \prime}(x)=e^{x}+(x-3) e^{x}=(x-2) e^{x}\)
\(f^{\prime \prime}(x)>0\) for \(x>2\)
\(f^{\prime}(x)<0\) for \(0<x<3\)
Therefore, the graph of \(f\) is both decreasing and concave up on the interval \(2<x<3\).
\item[(c)] \(f(3)=f(1)+\int_{1}^{3} f^{\prime}(x) d x=7+\int_{1}^{3}(x-3) e^{x} d x\)
\[
\begin{array}{cc}
u=x-3 & d v=e^{x} d x \\
d u=d x & v=e^{x}
\end{array}
\]
\[
\begin{aligned}
f(3) & =7+\left.(x-3) e^{x}\right|_{1} ^{3}-\int_{1}^{3} e^{x} d x \\
& =7+\left.\left((x-3) e^{x}-e^{x}\right)\right|_{1} ^{3} \\
& =7+3 e-e^{3}
\end{aligned}
\]
\end{itemize}

4: \(\left\{\begin{array}{l}1 \text { : uses initial condition } \\ 2: \text { integration by parts } \\ 1: \text { answer }\end{array}\right.\)
